<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<sdf version='1.9'>
  <model name='nose_cam'>
    <!--
      Derived from PX4's mono_cam. THE PARAMETERS WERE CHANGED because
      mono_cam does not represent our hardware:

                     mono_cam        nose_cam (real hardware)
        HFOV         1.74 rad = 99.7 0.8950 rad = 51.28 deg
        resolution   640x480         1920x1080

      ==========================================================
      THE REAL CAMERA - NO LONGER AN ASSUMPTION (confirmed 2026-08-05)
      ==========================================================
        Model       : DFM 37UR0234-ML (The Imaging Source)
        Sensor      : onsemi AR0234CS CMOS
        Format      : 1/2.6"
        Resolution  : 1920 x 1200
        Pixel       : 3.0 um
        Effective area (per the vendor) : 5.62 mm x 3.20 mm
        Lens        : 6 mm

      An earlier session ASSUMED "6 mm + 2 MP -> 1/2.6 inch (AR0234)".
      THE ASSUMPTION TURNED OUT TO BE RIGHT, so every range and geometry
      measurement taken before this confirmation still stands.

      === Why is the HFOV 0.8950 rad? ===
      HFOV = 2 * atan(sensor_width / (2 * focal_length))
           = 2 * atan(5.76 / (2 * 6)) = 51.28 deg = 0.8950 rad

      ⚠️ WHY 5.76 AND NOT THE VENDOR'S 5.62:
      The vendor's own page is SELF-CONTRADICTORY -
          1920 px x 3.0 um = 5.76 mm  (vendor says 5.62)
          1200 px x 3.0 um = 3.60 mm  (vendor says 3.20)
          1080 px x 3.0 um = 3.24 mm  <- "3.20" is far closer to this
      So the "effective area" is probably not the full 1920x1200 array.
      The renderer's pinhole must be consistent with the pixel geometry:
      gz samples 1920 columns, each 3.0 um -> 5.76 mm.

      THE SIZE AND DIRECTION OF THE UNCERTAINTY (this is what matters):
          5.76 mm -> 51.28 deg
          5.62 mm -> 50.19 deg     a difference of only 1.09 deg (2.1 %)
      A wider HFOV means more world fits into the same 1920 pixels, so the
      target looks SMALLER. Using 51.28 when the truth is 50.19 therefore
      makes the QR appear 2.1 % smaller -> it UNDERESTIMATES the decode
      range. The error is on the SAFE side. The measured 40 m decode
      threshold is unaffected (it was measured in 10 m steps).
      THE DEFINITIVE ANSWER: once the camera is in hand, MEASURE it with a
      chessboard and OpenCV calibrateCamera. This is not a datasheet
      argument to be won, it is a measurement to be taken.

      === Why 1080 rows and not 1200? ===
      The sensor is 1920x1200 = 16:10. The rulebook (p.12) allows only 4:3,
      5:4 and 16:9. 16:10 IS NOT ON THE LIST -> the judge video HAS TO be
      16:9. So the readout is cropped to 1080 rows (60 rows dropped from the
      top and 60 from the bottom).
      The consequence: the vertical field of view is
          VFOV = 2*atan(tan(HFOV/2)*1080/1920) = 30.22 deg
          (the full sensor would give 33.40 deg - which we cannot use)

      The rulebook (p.10) requires the camera to be mounted in the nose,
      parallel to and fixed with respect to the aircraft's longitudinal
      axis. -> pose 0 0 0 0 0 0 (aligned with the body axis), nose-mounted.
    -->
    <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
    <self_collide>false</self_collide>
    <static>false</static>
    <link name="nose_cam/base_link">
      <inertial>
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>0.050</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.00004</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.00004</iyy><iyz>0</iyz><izz>0.00004</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <sensor name="imager" type="camera">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <camera>
          <!-- 2*atan(5.76/12) = 0.895045 rad = 51.28 deg. The previous
               value was 0.8954 (a difference of 0.023 deg, which is 0.03 px
               on a 70 px QR - no measurable effect; it was corrected only so
               that it matches the derivation above exactly). -->
          <horizontal_fov>0.8950</horizontal_fov>
          <image>
            <width>1920</width>
            <height>1080</height>
            <format>R8G8B8</format>
          </image>
          <clip>
            <near>0.1</near>
            <far>3000</far>
          </clip>
        </camera>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>30</update_rate>
        <visualize>true</visualize>
        <topic>camera</topic>
      </sensor>
      <gravity>true</gravity>
      <velocity_decay/>
    </link>
  </model>
</sdf>
